  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 339 AM EDT WED OCT 7 2026\n\nThe extended period opens with quiet conditions, fair skies, and\nlight winds through the remainder of the work week as surface \nhigh pressure remains near eastern Kentucky through Thursday \nbefore starting to shift towards the Mid-Atlantic and Northeast on\nFriday. This will keep dry weather in place across the region, \nwith unseasonably warm afternoon highs reaching the upper 70s to \nlower 80s on both Thursday and Friday, with Friday a couple of\ndegrees warmer than Thursday. Clear skies and light winds will \nfavor ridge-valley temperature splits each night. Temperatures \neach night should drop into the mid-to-upper 40s in the coldest, \nsheltered valleys, while remaining in the 50s elsewhere, again\nwith Friday night a few degrees warmer than Thursday night.\n\nAll eyes turn to Tropical Depression Nine and the likelihood for a\nmajor weather system to impact the Deep South to the TN Valley and\nSouthern Appalachians this weekend. Current NHC forecasts show\nTropical Depression Nine strengthening to a hurricane before\nmaking landfall along the north-central Gulf Coast Friday evening.\nModels then show the potential for rapid deepening of this upper-\nlevel feature as strong non-linear heat/energy transfer occurs\nfrom the lower to upper levels over MS and vicinity during the day\nSaturday, before the remnant extratropical system moves northeast\nacross the TN and OH Valley regions late Saturday night into \nSunday.\n\nThe likely mutual amplification of the low-level and upper-level \nsystems will likely allow for the storm to maintain a well-defined\nsurface low well inland as it moves north toward the southern TN \nValley, and this will have implications on the forecast for the \nSouthern Appalachians and eastern Kentucky, as models depict a \nfairly strong pressure gradient developing between the northward- \nmoving surface system and the surface high/ridging over the Mid- \nAtlantic region beginning possibly as early Saturday morning and \nlasting into Saturday night. This most likely means a period of \ngusty winds developing over the Cumberland Mountain ridgetops \nFriday night into Saturday, with daytime mixing Saturday allowing \nfor breezy conditions potentially impacting lower elevations \nSaturday into Saturday night. Current modeling show max wind \ngusts possibly reaching or exceeding 40 mph at higher elevations, \nwith some potential for 25 to 40 mph to reach lower elevations, \nespecially closer to the TN border.\n\nRainfall amounts are highly uncertain, but the low-level \nsoutheasterly flow should significantly minimize heavy rainfall \nimpacts to the immediate north and west of the Cumberland \nMountains. This morning's daily WPC Excessive Rainfall Outlooks \nonly have a Marginal Risk for Saturday and Saturday night to the \nwest of Interstate 75, though brief periods of heavier rainfall \nwill certainly be possible as the system passes during this time. \n\nRain chances will diminish quickly from west to east Sunday into\nSunday night behind the departing system, with ridging building in\nfor Monday. Attention then turns to a cold front in northwesterly\nflow aloft that likely approaches the area for Tuesday. Fair \nskies will allow temperatures to warm into the upper 70s Tuesday \nahead of the cold front passage, while overnight lows will mostly \nbe in the mid to upper 50s from Saturday night through Monday \nnight.",
